      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Magnitude of a Vector-Valued Function",
      "problem": "Given that $r(t)=\u27e8cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}),\\,\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-4},\\,ln\\,(2\\,{e}^{t-2}-1)\u27e9,$ what is $|r(2)|?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$|r(2)|=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Evaluating the function at $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =\u27e8cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}),\\,\\sqrt{{2}^{3}-4},\\,ln\\,(2\\,{e}^{0}-1)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi ),\\,\\sqrt{4},\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-1,\\,2,\\,0\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(2)| & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
